        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          Expedition 71 began April 6, 2024, upon the departure of the Soyuz MS-24 (70S) from the International Space Station (“ISS,” “the station”).<div><br></div><div>Transitioning from Expedition 70 to Expedition 71 were Commander Oleg Kononenko (RSA) and Flight Engineers Michael Barratt (NASA), Tracy Caldwell-Dyson (NASA), Nicolai Chub (RSA), Matthew Dominick (NASA), Jeanette Epps (NASA), and Alexander Grebenkin (RSA).<div><br></div><div>While operating and maintaining the station's systems, the Expedition 71 crew continued to operate the many science experiments in progress as well as initiating new investigations and undertaking technology demonstrations.<div><br></div><div>The first of four planned spacewalks took place on April 25. Kononenko and Chub deployed a panel to a synthetic radar communication system and installed equipment needed to study corrosion on ISS surfaces and modules. The Russian spacewalk was completed in 4 hours and 36 minutes, nearly two hours ahead of schedule. On June 24, 2024, Dyson and Barratt began the second planned spacewalk, US EVA 90 (RFG Retrieve 2.5), expecting to remove an ailing electronics box from a communications antenna and to collect samples of microorganisms from the station’s exterior for later study. A water leak developed in the service and cooling umbilical unit on Dyson’s spacesuit, and the spacewalk was terminated after 31 minutes. Its tasks and those of the two remaining planned spacewalks, US EVA 91 (CP9 EHDC R&R), and US EVA 92 (IROSA Prep 2A), were rescheduled for later missions.<div><br></div><div>The Expedition 71 crew was the first to welcome the crew of a Boeing CST-100 Starliner to the ISS. Crewed by NASA astronauts Barry "Butch" Wilmore (Commander) and Sunita "Suni" Williams (Pilot) the Boeing Crewed Flight Test (Boe-CFT) mission launched on June 5, 2024, docking autonomously to the station on June 6. Boe-CFT was expected to last about 8 days. However, technical issues occurring during the flight to the station delayed its scheduled return. Subsequently, a decision was made to return the Starliner home uncrewed as a safety precaution. Boe-CFT undocked and returned home autonomously and safely on September 6. Wilmore and Williams remained aboard the station, no longer “visiting crew,” but formally joining the Expedition 71 crew.<div><br></div><div>As a result of their mission extension, crew rotation plans for the upcoming transition to Expedition 72 were adjusted.  SpaceX Crew-9 was expected to launch and dock in mid-August but was delayed until Expedition 72.  SpaceX Crew-8, including Dominick, Barratt, Epps and Grebenkin, was slated to undock and return to Earth in late August but was also delayed until Expedition 72.<div><br></div><div>On September 11 Soyuz MS-26 (72S) launched and docked, bringing Aleksey Ovchinin (RSA), Ivan Vagner (RSA) and Donald Pettit (NASA) to ISS.<div><br></div><div>Then, on September 22, Kononenko turned command of the station over to Williams in preparation for leaving the station.<div><div><br></div><div>The following day, Kononenko and crewmates Chub and Caldwell-Dyson boarded their Soyuz MS-25 (71S) for their return to trip home, undocking and landing on September 23.<div><br></div><div>With the undocking of MS-25, Expedition 71 ended.
<div><br></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div>
